1 | INTRODUCTION
The COVID-19 pandemic brought numerous changes in higher education, moving from in-person meetings to online collaborations and remote
work (Katz, 2021). Research studies with in-person interactions were paused or discontinued or shifted to remote data collection, where possible
(Nomali et al., 2023), and laboratory research was disrupted (Sohrabi et al., 2021). Adverse effects on student careers during this time were
reported, for example, challenges with pursuing research at home (Sifri et al., 2022), less access to faculty or difficulties with job searches
(Katz, 2021). Longer term consequences on research and training programmes are unclear (Sohrabi et al., 2021). While biomedical statistical con
sulting units at medical centers may have seen an increase in demand due to COVID-related analyses (Lee et al., 2022), there was a reduction in
the number of projects at other academic statistical consulting centers (Niu, 2023).
