Graduate students faced limited resources and heightened uncertainty navigating their programmes amidst the pandemic's disruptions.
Statistical consulting units can provide guidance to graduate students at critical junctures of their academic journeys. However, experiences of
graduate student consultants and clients from different research fields regarding research support, work characteristics and training in academic
statistical consulting centers have not been studied.
Every statistical consulting and collaboration unit (SCC) is unique (Vance, 2015). The names vary from ‘statistical consulting center’ and ‘data science
consulting and collaboration units’ to ‘statistical lab’ among many other terms. Most SCCs are open-ended in supporting research involving data ranging
from software support to guidance on study design and guiding or executing data analysis. Some SCCs have training components offering seminars or
workshops. Mentoring graduate student consultants and supporting graduate student research is a key mission for many SCCs. A thriving SCC develops
partnerships and networks across the institution, for example, with multidisciplinary teams, individual departments or colleges who seek to provide their
members with access to statistical expertise (Huebner et al., 2023). One such partnership may be with the Graduate School to facilitate building capacity
in statistical and computational skills of graduate students working on their theses or dissertations, to provide opportunities for graduate students to
become expert consultants and collaborators or to support educational events, especially for responsible conduct of research in data science.
The purpose of this paper is to describe experiences in an academic SCC related to graduate students, both as consultants and clients, to
highlight their experiences and needs during the COVID-19 pandemic and to provide recommendations for future practices.
This is in the case study of Michigan State University's Center for Statistical Training and Consulting and includes experiences from RAs who were
employed before and during the COVID-19 pandemic. In our SCC, full-time staff and doctoral graduate students are available for consulting and col
laborations year-round. Collaborations are sought by hundreds of researchers (faculty, staff and graduate students) across the university every year.
In Section 2, we describe appeals and barriers to seeking out and utilizing statistical support by graduate students. In Section 3, we discuss
onboarding and mentoring of new RAs pre COVID-19 and changes during the pandemic. Experiences of graduate students about research sup
port, degree progress, work characteristics and collaborations are described in Section 4. Finally, we conclude with practical suggestions for an
SCC to support graduate students.
2 | ORGANIZATION AND UTILIZATION OF THE SCC
The mission of the Center for Statistical Training and Consulting at Michigan State University is to improve quality and impact of research, to help
scholars and their research programmes thrive and to increase scientific rigour and reproducibility. The SCC provides expertise and guidance on
study design, statistical methods, interpretation of results, data management, conducting statistical analyses and writing reproducible reports.
While the Department of Statistics and Probability started a statistical consulting service in the 1980s, resulting from these efforts the SCC was
established in 2004 with an administrative home in the Office of Research and Innovation (Gilliland & Stapleton, 2013). SCC staff consists of full
time statisticians (employed in a continuing system rather than fixed term), doctoral student RAs and administrative support staff. The services
are usually free for individuals affiliated with sponsoring units and fee-for-service for others. The SCC partners with The Graduate School so that
SCC statisticians can provide free support to graduate students working on their theses and dissertations to conduct appropriate statistical ana
lyses, interpret the results correctly and help them learn about important statistical methods and tools to advance their professional development.
Graduate students seeking statistical support for their research projects account for 35% to 40% of SCC's cases on average.
Graduate students have access to statistical support with a set number of hours per year as well as opportunities to attend seminars or work
shops offered by the SCC. The number of available hours to student clients differs by college. Fees apply for continued support beyond those
hours. Some colleges sponsor the SCC with an annual fixed contribution or with a percent full-time equivalent (FTE) and thus provide free access
to statistical services for their members. Information about SCC services is made known through communication channels in colleges, graduate
school newsletters, SCC staff participating in resource fairs or presenting at student seminars or through the SCC website and SCC emails. A com
munication manager to help with advertising and effective dissemination of events, opportunities and services is not available at this time.
2.1 | Appeals and barriers to utilizing the services of the SCC
In Fall 2021, faculty and graduate students were asked to provide feedback in an anonymous survey conducted to understand their quantitative
needs that the SCC could help with. While this survey was given during the pandemic, the questions were not specific to this period. Results were
reported in summary form on the SCC website and reproduced here (https://cstat.msu.edu/about). Survey questions included ‘What attracts you
to work with [SCC] statisticians or utilize [SCC] services?’ and ‘What barriers make it harder for you to work with [SCC] statisticians or utilize
[SCC] services?’ with a Likert scale from strongly agree to strongly disagree. The response options for the question ‘What are your statistical
training needs?’ included topics listed on the SCC website.
The overwhelming majority of graduate student survey respondents (Table 1) appreciate advice on statistical methods (81.1%), SCC staff help
conducting the analysis (70.1%) and educational offerings (80.3%). Graduate students would like to see seminars or workshops on statistical
methods (78.7%) followed by training on statistical software (58.3%) and data visualization (59.1%). The largest barrier noted by graduate student
