2.2 | Pandemic impact on utilization of SCC services
SCC staff members continued to work remotely following the lockdown on 12 March 2020. The number of open cases from graduate student
research projects decreased (Figure 1). SCC's policy is to close cases when there is a long halt, and clients are invited to submit new cases when
they are ready to restart, for more transparent consultant case load and limiting potential scope creep. Brief downturns are expected when stu
dents graduate and leave the university at the end of Spring semester, but the decline was more pronounced in Spring 2020 compared to the
same period in 2021. By the end of Fall 2020, the case counts had recovered and have since exceeded prior counts. This is in part due to new
support from the Graduate School that started in May 2020 and due to new partnerships with the medical colleges that grew over time. This addi
tional support facilitated graduate students' research during the lockdown as is illustrated by the fact that the proportion of cases from graduate
students increased compared to all open cases even during the lockdown (Figure 2).
