The weekly staff meetings provide additional learning opportunities for new and continuing RAs. They are asked to summarize a case they
are working on and the approach they are taking, followed by a team discussion about relevant issues, alternative methods and offering pointers
towards resources such as methodology literature. RAs are encouraged to participate in discussions of other consultants' cases and may request a
discussion of a particular case they have questions about. These activities help consultants hone their communication and problem-solving skills
and expand their knowledge of applied statistics.
3.2 | Pandemic impact on onboarding and mentoring of RAs
Organizational changes during the COVID-19 pandemic included conducting weekly staff meetings online and mentoring and onboarding new
staff such as graduate student RAs via videoconferencing instead of in-person meetings. The SCC needed more computing equipment for home
offices and staff expanded their skills regarding effective use of technology.
Our pre-pandemic process for training and mentoring RAs relied heavily on in-person contact. Physical proximity in the office facilitated many
informal mentoring exchanges between the supervisor and the RAs, peer support and camaraderie among the RAs. During and after the COVID
19 pandemic, most newly hired RAs were trained remotely. While the mentoring topics did not change, some approaches to disseminating specific
skills and strategies to RAs were constrained. For example, statistical approaches are often readily taught sketching conceptual models, listing rel
evant variables or writing equations on boards; our in-person staff meetings thrived with such tried-and-true tools. When training new SCC hires
remotely, this teaching strategy was more difficult because digital whiteboards were unfamiliar, drawing with a mouse is often clumsier than with
a physical pen and limited screen space makes it hard to see the whiteboard and other meeting participants at the same time. Mentoring did not
include topics on effective videoconferencing other than providing feedback on audio quality or lighting, and SCC staff learned skills on their own
and shared experiences.
The vastly increased use of remote work induced by the pandemic raised some policy issues we had not dealt with before. Regarding the geo
graphic location, we settled on allowing RAs to work remotely from other places within the state temporarily and initially made limited exceptions
for RAs located elsewhere in the United States, since some students moved home. The latter poses a barrier partly because time zone differences
make scheduling more difficult or results in mix-ups, and distance sometimes adds lag to network connections. Concerns about export control reg
ulations led us to prohibit working from outside the United States. During the pandemic, university policies were developed or revised and cov
ered rules about remote work.
4 | SCC ENVIRONMENT AND RESEARCH SUPPORT FOR GRADUATE STUDENTS
A collaborative and respectful environment is of utmost importance for a thriving SCC in any circumstances. Graduate students benefit not only
from research support but also from career support. This applies both to RAs and clients. Graduate student clients learn to apply appropriate sta
tistical methods for their research projects, discover new approaches and get support to negotiate such approaches with their advisors and com
mittees. RAs broaden their repertoire of statistical methods, explore statistical approaches for their own research projects, build networks and
improve communication skills. They learn to manage multiple projects and hone their organizational and note taking skills. These skills transfer
when they prepare for job applications and interviews. Such support is independent of the COVID-19 pandemic, but collaborations and work
characteristics changed, and new community norms emerged.
4.1 | Research support and exploring statistical methods
Graduate students seek statistical support from the SCC for their research projects. More than 80% of survey participants responded that advice
on statistical methods is the main appeal of the SCC (Table 1). Clients are matched with consultants who explain options for study designs and
statistical analysis approaches.
