and statistical methods because of the varied caseload they support.
The SCC fosters an environment of intellectual growth, empowering RAs with the freedom to explore and develop new areas of interest. As
RAs expand their repertoire of statistical methodologies through their work at the SCC, they not only apply these newly acquired methods to
enhance their own research but also disseminate knowledge by sharing their expertise with graduate student clients.
I remember one of my clients jokingly pleaded with me to hold off my graduation till she graduated so I could help her navigate the
issues she was having with her dissertation. According to her, she was not getting sufficient methodology guidance with her advi
sor but her consultations with the [SCC] was helping to fill that gap.
Graduate student clients may adopt newly learned statistical methods in their current and subsequent research projects and may introduce
these methods in their fields of study.
After I successfully changed my client's methodological approach, he came back a few months later with a new project. He wanted
me to help review his R script and address reviewers' comments for the tutorial paper he wrote applying the approach in his field. I
was impressed by how much time he had devoted to studying this method and discussing its relevance for his field of study. I pro
vided the requested help, and the paper was later published.
By responding to reviewers' comments on conference and journal articles, RAs learn about pitfalls in academic writing while honing their own
research communication skills.
It takes time to get an adequate understanding of the client's research objectives, context, study design and data. Initial meetings that include
the graduate student and their advisor can help speed up this process. Often issues must be addressed above and beyond what the student ini
tially identified. There may be multiple options to discuss with their comparative merits and flaws. Once a statistical analysis plan has been identi
fied, continued help may be needed to implement such a plan. Suggesting a plan that the student cannot execute leads to frustration, anxiety and
dissatisfaction. We suggest resources to facilitate building the student's knowledge and capacity to apply it. Advice on structuring the data
and learning its properties in a systematic manner should accompany guidance regarding the statistical models. Such tasks may need to be com
pleted before further meetings to inform the client and the consultant before deciding on next steps. At the same time, this process often requires
the RA to do background reading and hone their own capacity and coaching skills. They guide the client to compose a coherent plan on how to
solve the problem or think about ways to deal with potential obstacles. The time for robust statistical analysis and data visualization is often over
looked as a cost in grant projects. Students have shared their concern of needing subsidization beyond the available free hours of SCCs support.
Such financial constraints can be a substantial barrier to utilizing SCCs services (Table 1).
4.2 | Navigating challenges with degree progress
Graduate students feel they need to make independent progress and be prepared to defend the decisions without the benefit of informal check
ins with their committee members to discuss ideas before proceeding. The SCC creates a safe space for exploring analysis ideas in consulting
meetings. Explaining options for study designs and statistical analysis approaches is one of the key tasks for RAs. RAs are in a unique position to
observe the dissertation process through the experiences of their graduate student clients, providing them with valuable foresight and knowledge
as they work on their own degrees. RAs experience that the consulting they provide and mentoring they receive at the SCC expands their
knowledge of statistical tools that may creatively reshape and enhance research outcomes for their dissertations and equips them to engage more
productively with their advisors.
Sometimes students may mention conflicts with an advisor or committee, including lack of availability to give advice or lack of awareness of
the work the student has already completed. This hinders students from attaining their goals or makes them feel less valued or respected. Lack
of interactions negatively impact research outcomes and timelines associated with completing essential tasks. RAs may be perceived as confi
dants, and student clients may describe these and related challenges, especially discord concerning analytical approaches to them. RAs are
encouraged to discuss cases to get advice and input from the team in the weekly staff meetings or from SCC supervisors, who can step in to join
meetings with clients as needed. The SCC invited the university ombudsperson to a staff meeting to help with awareness and learn about
resources that can then be passed along to students.
