4.3 | Pandemic impact on collaborations and videoconferencing
The abrupt shift from in-person to remote consulting during the lockdown presented substantial challenges. Not all consultants and
clients possessed a home office environment conducive to regular virtual meetings. This led to initial delays and difficulties as individuals
set up the necessary equipment and familiarized themselves with video conferencing tools. Unreliable internet connections occasionally
interrupted video or audio streams, forcing meeting participants to repeat themselves, exit the meeting and reconnect or even postpone
meetings.
The SCC started a lending programme of laptops for RAs. The continuity and support provided by the SCC played a vital role in helping RAs
through this period of transition, although the success of the transition to online meetings with clients varied.
Videoconferencing with clients were not as smooth because clients were at varying levels of technological familiarity.
Despite the initial hurdles, consultants found some positive aspects in switching to remote meetings. The transition demanded creative
solutions to maintain the quality and effectiveness of client engagements, not only navigating the obstacles imposed by the pandemic but also
spurring the adoption of tools and techniques that are useful for both remote and in-person meetings going forward.
Initially, in-person meetings with my client involved walking her through how to use SPSS for her analysis using verbal descriptions
and on-screen guidance, with the client taking notes on the process. The transition from in-person to remote sessions posed some
challenges to this method (for instance, I could not physically point to where to click on her screen), so we had to adjust our
approach. Using Zoom, I could request control of the client's screen, enabling real-time demonstrations while the client recorded
the session. This adaptation proved much more efficient, allowing me to provide explanations and address questions throughout
the process, while providing the client with a valuable reference tool for future use.
RAs found camaraderie and support from both SCC colleagues and clients as they collectively adapted to the unique challenges of conducting
research during the pandemic.
After a few Zoom meetings, we established a rapport and, given we were both working on our dissertations then, we turned the
Zoom meeting to a study room, with each person working on her own thesis. With this arrangement, she could unmute herself
from time to time to ask for help on her work. The lockdown and use of Zoom had created a less formal connection between us
that went beyond the [SCC]. To date, this client, now an assistant professor in a university, still joins me on Zoom from her office
so provide each other with virtual company while working on our separate job tasks.
The virtual consultation during and post COVID pandemic has made consulting meetings more accessible for busy advisors and allowed them
to attend the meetings together with their student more frequently. This not only empowered the student client but also provided a professional
development platform for the RAs. RAs can learn from the domain expertise of the advisors, which may lead to future collaborations beyond the
SCC activities.
The mentoring and consulting sessions not only improved my statistical skills but also opened several networking opportunities for
me. Through my consulting sessions, I learned the expertise of the students and faculty members I worked with and found some
that were relevant to my own dissertation work. Eventually, my own dissertation committee included a previous faculty consulting
client, and an [SCC] alum. After I graduated, the same faculty member invited me to serve as a dissertation committee member for
one of her graduate students who was a RA at the [SCC].
However, explaining problems can take longer during a virtual meeting than during an in-person meeting for both clients and consultants.
