4.4 | Work characteristics and collaborations
The SCC fosters a respectful and inclusive work environment. Our core values were developed with input from all staff members and graduate
students. The collaborative environment enables RAs to engage in meaningful discussions about their consulting projects as well as their own
research. These discussions are greatly enriched by the diverse academic backgrounds and perspectives of their fellow consultants. The diversity
of client projects and associated programmes begets diversity of client-RA interactions, both in terms of scientific topics and disciplines as well as
socioeconomic backgrounds, thus enriching the collaborative research experience and encouraging professional growth.
Pre-pandemic video consulting was sporadic and initiated due to clients who could not easily attend meetings on campus. Such cases often
depended on constraints related to the clients' resources, time and abilities to travel from one end of a large campus to another, where the SCC
was housed. A few instances relied on video consulting to continue client-consultant collaborations during travel. Video consulting was not a tool
that our team used regularly before the pandemic. Switching to screen-sharing interactions and associated lessons and materials was not seamless
for most consultants and their clients. Interactions between staff and clients became less personable, especially when client or consultant
participants chose to keep their videoconferencing cameras turned off. With time and practice, amidst a growing demand for consulting services,
client-consultant pairs adapted and honed their technology skills and became more patient and understanding. There were fewer opportunities to
informally interact with the other RAs for peer support and camaraderie between meetings.
The switch to fully remote staff meetings was smoother for me than it was for my other job commitments during the lockdown.
Remote staff meetings were fine at first because I thought it was just going to last for a short while, but, with time, the impersonal
nature of this form of meeting started to tell on me.
To help mitigate the lack of interaction, new informal, but virtual, monthly meetings between the SCC director and RAs were introduced.
They provided opportunities to chat about client cases, their own research, personal experiences or anything else they wanted to discuss.
Conducting meetings online involves screen sharing and using a whiteboard, but this approach was neither seamless nor natural, particularly
compared to in-person instruction and use of a board to sketch out key concepts. Instruction through videoconferencing can make the client
consultant experience and relationship more difficult to navigate and accurately gauge the understanding of clients in need of help and virtual
tools often lengthened the time for both clients and consultants to explain problems, teach new skills and enhance conceptual understandings.
Anecdotally, the increased use of videoconferencing may have positively correlated with increased numbers of clients missed, postponed or can
celled meetings. Such occurrences seemed related to clients lacking traditional time and resource investments such as leaving home to travel to
work and thus commuting between academic buildings to accomplish various tasks. However, the convenience of online scheduling and virtual
instruction enabled faster project-turnaround times, recordings of instructional sessions for subsequent client and consultant review and
enhanced learning, and the effective use of available technology improved over time as described in the following two experiences.
The flexibility in scheduling meetings online enabled a more efficient workflow with clients. Before COVID, when my client had a
quick question or issue that impeded their progress, they typically saved it for our next meeting or I would try to assist them via
email, which can be challenging. It was usually impractical to arrange a short meeting the same day due to the need for the client
to travel across campus to [SCC]. However, after transitioning to online meetings, we were often able to schedule a brief session
within a few hours to address the issue, enabling the client to continue to make progress on their work with minimal disruptions.
The convenience of remote work was appreciated.
I could attend meetings with my [SCC] clients, work on my dissertation, and meet the responsibilities of my new job all without
leaving my desk.
Now online consulting meetings are the most popular choice. Our intake form for new projects includes an item asking about preferences for
meeting modality. More than 50% request online meetings (a higher proportion of 70% coinciding with graduation deadlines), 6% in-person and
the remaining do not indicate a preference. However, when an in-person meeting is offered, they invariably also choose the online option. This is
related to the desire of clients with demanding daily work schedules to minimize their time away from labs or clinics or reduce productivity costs
spent travelling or finding parking.
The mentoring and consulting format at the SCC provide RAs with many career and personal development opportunities including workflow
and project management skills. For some projects, RAs negotiate fee-for-service bids. To be successful in the bid process, RAs get instructions on
how to categorize the work required for a case and estimate the time effort needed for each of these categories. They agree on a statement of
work with their clients and negotiate the associated cost estimates (Peterson et al., 2022). These are important skills for future grant writing and
preparing for careers.
