4.5 | Educational opportunities
Seminars and workshops moved online and thus were more accessible across the university due to the convenience of attending remotely. SCC
seminar offerings were expanded due to the overwhelming interest expressed in the survey (Table 2) and now included presentations by interna
tionally renowned statisticians, as well as additional summer seminar series with specific themes (Reporting Guidelines, Data Ethics, Open Science,
Data Visualization), and introducing additional topics related to responsible conduct of research (RCR). The attendance in SCC seminars doubled
compared to pre-pandemic years, with a 58% increase in participation from the natural sciences, engineering and agriculture; a 25% increase in
social sciences, education and communication; and 84% increase from the medical colleges. It also allowed participation of international students
who were unable to travel or advisees of the presenters at other universities. Despite the interest in educational offerings on statistical methods
or software (Table 2), the number of 3-h workshops was greatly reduced due to the time commitment for SCC staff in preparing materials and lack
of interest with respect to number of participants willing or able to commit this time.
The RCR seminars continue to be offered with topics related to Rigor and Reproducibility and Data Management in quantitative sciences.
Before receiving credit, students are required to answer questions about the seminar content via an online survey. This is also an opportunity to
provide feedback and request additional topics of interest. RCR seminars are especially appreciated since they give more opportunities through
out the year in addition to what is offered by the Graduate School and due to being more in-depth about RCR practices in the quantitative sci
ences as illustrated by some of the feedback. Students comment that this covers new information and that they find this more applicable to their
own research than the typical topics repeated each year in RCR workshops. For example, students may not be encouraged by their research group
or get the time and support to do a thorough data cleaning process and to understand data properties before rushing to sophisticated analyses.
There is a lack of knowledge about implications for the interpretation of results and generalizability because of excluding data samples, overfitting,
p hacking or ad hoc decisions during the statistical analysis process are some of the topics that are often not covered in their curriculum.
5 | DISCUSSION AND OUTLOOK
The COVID-19 outbreak brought many changes to the research community and the workflow of statistical consulting centers. College students
struggled with social distancing (Hamlin & Barney, 2022) and researchers experienced more isolation during the COVID-19 pandemic
(Katz, 2021). In a survey of chemistry departments, graduate students and postdoctoral fellows reported that the quality of collaborations
decreased, resulting in less interactions, lack of communication and reduced collaborations (Sifri et al., 2022). A statistical consulting center where
the main consultants are graduate students taking consulting courses during semesters reported the lowest number of cases in Fall 2021 and
recovery to pre-pandemic levels by Spring 2022 (Niu, 2023). Our SCC is continuously staffed throughout the year by RAs and full-time staff. We
observed a steady decrease in the number of cases by graduate students in the months following the lockdown, but the case counts had recov
ered by the end of Fall 2020. The importance of at least some free access to statistical consulting is evidenced by the increasing proportion of
graduate student projects in the SCCs portfolio when this was made possible due to the Graduate School's support. For the SCC's sustainability
and growth and for high quality research outcomes, it is essential that funding sources are made available for university-specific priorities, such as
providing statistical support and training to students and faculty across the university or support for grant proposal development (Huebner
et al., 2023).
New community norms for researchers emerged during the pandemic including a desire and openness for remote work and hybrid events
(Katz, 2021; Niu, 2023). At our SCC, most meeting modalities are now requests for online meetings and remote collaborations. Telecommuting
and hybrid staff meetings support flexible personal schedules and needs, but too much screen time can be exhausting (Trott et al., 2022).
Irrespective of the COVID era graduate students value guidance for research and degree progress, help with achieving their career goals, and
respectful and inclusive work environments (Sifri et al., 2022). A statistical consultant has multiple roles: expert professional, communicator, col
laborator, leader, advocate and scholar (Zapf et al., 2019). RAs learn skills to listen carefully and to ask questions, explain statistical methods and
their usage and empower their clients to build their capacity (Vance & Smith, 2019). Clients gain a deeper understanding of statistical methods,
appropriately interpret the results and explain and justify their choices to their advisors and committee members. RAs in the SCC fulfil dual roles
of supporting graduate student clients while having to deal with their own stresses regarding completion of their degree and job searches.
Based on our experiences, we offer suggestions for SCC practices to benefit graduate student clients and consultants.
1. Environment: a respectful and inclusive work environment is of utmost importance for a thriving SCC. It provides RAs with career and personal
development opportunities. An SCC can be a ‘safe space’ for graduate students to explore statistical methods for their research ideas. The
experiences directly benefit RAs by enhancing their own pursuits of producing high-quality, collaborative research. Furthermore, a quality
scholarly environment attracts clients and new opportunities for collaboration, a lifeline for great business and strong community.
2. Staff meetings: in-person staff meetings are highly valuable for collaborations, brainstorming sessions and social interactions, but hybrid meet
ings should also be an occasional option. Regular discussion of potential challenges in projects, including advantages and disadvantages of
