3. Online meetings and remote collaborations: many graduate student clients choose this option to reduce commuting time or to be able to fit
their busy schedules and disparate locations. It can result in a faster turnaround for answering questions, scheduling meetings and completing
projects. The convenience of recording online meetings allows for later review and better understanding of points that may have been missed
in a densely packed advising session. It is more feasible for advisors to attend the meetings. This empowers student clients by building
student-advisor relationships while working towards a common goal and provides opportunities for RAs to learn from domain experts and help
build collaborative interdisciplinary networks across the university. However, in-person meetings may be more productive to gauge under
standing, explain multiple approaches, discuss strategies, utilize physical teaching media or progress to next steps.
4. Mentoring RAs: sessions should be conducted in-person for a richer interactive experience and to build interpersonal relationships. In-person
mentoring can be augmented with quick online calls for problem solving and fast turnaround. Onboarding topics include mission and core
values for the SCC, the RA's role and expectations, ethical guidelines, statistical consulting practices, digital notetaking tools and analysis soft
ware, and communication and management skills. Guidance for conducting effective online meetings is both needed and useful, exposing new
staff to multiple modalities of teaching and learning. Observing other consultants through shadowing and having secondary consultants work
on their first projects are effective approaches to help RAs become expert consultants and collaborators.
5. Telecommuting: RAs have for more flexibility with remote work scheduling meetings, working on client projects or making progress on their
own research. The option of using office space in the SCC can provide welcome boundaries between work and personal lives. The opportunity
to meet with SCC peers and share ideas and get their feedback or to browse statistics books available at the SCC can provide major benefits.
Rules about telecommuting and about the expected number of in-person office days need to be carefully assessed and clearly communicated.
It is important for any organization to encourage in-person collaboration to foster communities of trust and engagement.
6. Technological support: a lending programme for equipment is useful to optimize RAs set-up for videoconferencing, assures proper installation
of approved analysis software and antivirus software and allows secured login to the SCC's server to run data analyses. Feedback on audio
quality, lighting, presentation or background helps with a good start for building good relationships with online clients. Sharing information
about SCC peers' software and hardware preferences can be useful for inspiring work efficiency.
7. Educational offerings: external speakers and local experts may be engaged as presenters to offer a broader range of topics in online seminars.
Responsible conduct of research in data management, statistical analysis and reporting are of interest to many students. In-person workshops
can be developed for more in-depth topics to learn skills that augment a student's curriculum and allow for interactions and individual atten
tion. Encouraging learning new skills, best practices and recent advances benefits RAs as well as the wider university community.
In summary, this paper helps with providing a better understanding of what matters to graduate student consultants and clients at an SCC as
it relates to research support, workplace characteristics and collaborations, and educational opportunities. The COVID-19 pandemic had an
impact on caseload and changes in approaches to academic mentoring and collaborations. Some of the changes are quickly becoming community
norms, such as receiving statistical support via videoconferencing or being able to attend online training and give presentations at considerable
distances from home or office. However, onboarding and mentoring of RAs should be conducted in person, and every effort should be made to
encourage in-person interactions. Environments play a key role in their professional development. Challenging, diverse, supportive and scholarly
workspaces beget high-quality SCCs, staff consultants, projects, collaborations and scientific publications.
